Notebook[{Cell[CellGroupData[{Cell["init", "ObjectName"], Cell[TextData[{Cell["   ", "ModInfo"], Cell[BoxData["init[opts]"], "InlineFormula"], " \:2028", "sets up a tight-binding model from its lattice, Wyckoff positions, symmetry, and local orbitals."}], "Usage"], Cell["The five main inputs have a direct physical meaning: lattice and lattpar define the Bravais lattice, wyckoffposition specifies the inequivalent sites and their moments, symminformation gives the symmetry operations, and basisFunctions specifies the local orbital space on each Wyckoff orbit.", "Notes"], Cell["After init finishes, first inspect the generated atoms, the orbital order, and the neighbour shells. The hopping parameters are not needed at this stage; symham determines them later, one shell at a time.", "Notes"], Cell["By default init finds the first 10 complete bond shells. If a model needs a farther shell, choose a larger InitialBondShells value and run init again; symham itself does not search for new bonds.", "Notes"], Cell["RepresentationMode selects how the full orbital representation is built. Use \"DirectProduct\" when the action on the complete local basis is known, and \"Induced\" when the input starts from a representation of a site-symmetry group.", "Notes"], Cell["Every call to init starts a new model. If the input is inconsistent, the calculation stops and asks the user to correct it; MagneticTB does not silently continue with the preceding model.", "Notes"], Cell[BoxData[GridBox[{{Cell["      ", "TableRowIcon"], "lattice", "{{a,0,0},{-a/2,Sqrt[3] a/2,0},{0,0,c}}", Cell["Primitive direct-lattice vectors stored by rows. After applying lattpar, the result must be a real, nonsingular numerical 3 by 3 matrix.", "TableText"]}, {Cell["      ", "TableRowIcon"], "lattpar", "{a->1,c->3}", Cell["Replacement rules that assign numerical values to every symbolic lattice parameter needed by lattice.", "TableText"]}, {Cell["      ", "TableRowIcon"], "wyckoffposition", "{{{2/3,1/3,0},{0,0,1/2}}}", Cell["One {fractional seed position, magnetic moment} pair for each Wyckoff orbit. The outer-list order also fixes the order of basisFunctions.", "TableText"]}, {Cell["      ", "TableRowIcon"], "symminformation", "{{\"1\",IdentityMatrix[3],{0,0,0},\"F\"}}", Cell["Either ordered magnetic-group records {label,R,t,\"F\"|\"T\"} or a discrete spin-space-group Association. The antiunitary flag belongs to each operation record.", "TableText"]}, {Cell["      ", "TableRowIcon"], "basisFunctions", "{\"s\"}", Cell["A nonempty local scalar-orbital or two-component spinor basis for every Wyckoff seed. Named orbitals and exact symbolic functions are accepted.", "TableText"]}, {Cell["      ", "TableRowIcon"], "InitialBondShells", "10", Cell["Number of complete bond shells found during initialization. Shell 1 contains the zero-distance onsite terms; shell 2 is the first nonzero-distance neighbour shell.", "TableText"]}, {Cell["      ", "TableRowIcon"], "GenerateSymmetryGroup", "False", Cell["False treats the supplied ordered records as the complete finite group. True closes supplied generators using the operation-specific multiplication law.", "TableText"]}, {Cell["      ", "TableRowIcon"], "RepresentationMode", "\"DirectProduct\"", Cell["\"DirectProduct\" uses the local action on the full basis together with the permutation of equivalent sites. \"Induced\" derives the reference-site representation from basisFunctions and transports it to the other sites. Use initfromrep when exact site-symmetry matrices are already known.", "TableText"]}}, GridBoxAlignment -> {"Columns" -> {{Left}}}, GridBoxDividers -> {"Rows" -> {{False}}}, GridBoxSpacings -> {"Columns" -> {Offset[0.27999999999999997], {Offset[0.7]}, Offset[0.27999999999999997]}, "Rows" -> {Offset[0.2], {Offset[0.8]}, Offset[0.2]}}]], "3ColumnTableMod"]}, Open]], Cell[CellGroupData[{Cell["Examples Initialization", "ExamplesInitializationSection"], Cell[BoxData["Needs[\"MagneticTB`\"]"], "ExampleInitialization"]}, Open]], Cell[CellGroupData[{Cell["Basic Examples", "PrimaryExamplesSection"], Cell["Initialize graphene with the complete ordered list returned by msgop:", "ExampleText"], Cell[BoxData["grapheneOperations = msgop[gray[191]];\ninit[\n  lattice -> {\n    {a, 0, 0},\n    {-a/2, Sqrt[3] a/2, 0},\n    {0, 0, c}\n  },\n  lattpar -> {a -> 1, c -> 3},\n  wyckoffposition -> {\n    {{1/3, 2/3, 0}, {0, 0, 0}}\n  },\n  symminformation -> grapheneOperations,\n  basisFunctions -> {{\"pz\"}}\n];"], "Input"], Cell["Inspect the Hamiltonian ordering. The two rows are the symmetry-related carbon sites generated from the single Wyckoff seed:", "ExampleText"], Cell[BoxData["orbitalTable[]"], "Input"], Cell[BoxData[TagBox[GridBox[{{TemplateBox[{"\"Hamiltonian dimension = \"", "2", "\"; representation mode = \"", "\"DirectProduct\""}, "RowDefault"], "\:f3ba", "\:f3ba", "\:f3ba", "\:f3ba", "\:f3ba", "\:f3ba", "\:f3ba", "\:f3ba", "\:f3ba", "\:f3ba", "\:f3ba"}, {"\"H index\"", "\"Site\"", "\"Wyckoff orbit\"", "\"Atom\"", "\"Fractional position\"", "\"Cartesian position\"", "\"Local orbital\"", "\"Input basis\"", "\"Actual basis state\"", "\"Spin structure\"", "\"Spin state\"", "\"Transport operation\""}, {"1", "1", "1", "1", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{FractionBox["1", "3"], ",", FractionBox["2", "3"], ",", "0"}], "}"}], RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"0", ",", FractionBox["1", SqrtBox["3"]], ",", "0"}], "}"}], "1", "\"pz\"", "z", "\"Spinless\"", "None", "None"}, {"2", "2", "1", "2", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{FractionBox["2", "3"], ",", FractionBox["1", "3"], ",", "0"}], "}"}], RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{FractionBox["1", "2"], ",", FractionBox["1", RowBox[{"2", " ", SqrtBox["3"]}]], ",", "0"}], "}"}], "1", "\"pz\"", "z", "\"Spinless\"", "None", "None"}}, GridBoxAlignment -> {"Columns" -> {{Left}}}, AutoDelete -> False, GridBoxFrame -> {"Columns" -> {{True}}, "Rows" -> {{True}}}, GridBoxItemSize -> {"Columns" -> {{Automatic}}, "Rows" -> {{Automatic}}}], "Grid"]], "Output"]}, Open]], Cell[CellGroupData[{Cell["More Examples", "Exten